OUT='/tmp/kh/extra/'
def F(name,cap,text,w='0.96'): return text.strip()+'\n\n\\appfig{%s}{%s}{%s}\n\n'%(name,w,cap)
HEAD=r"\subsection{The output of the application, panel by panel}"+"\n"
D={}
D[1]=HEAD+r"The water of Chapter 1 is not only a set of equations: in SWAFL it is a set of pictures that change with the settings of the user. The layer \emph{Soil hydraulic drivers} shows how the retention curve and the conductivity of the soil move when the tillage, the consolidation and the texture change, and the figures below are its panels for the coarse loam."+"\n\n"
D[1]+=F('v7_0',r"What drives change in soil hydraulic properties: the response of the soil to tillage, consolidation rate and organic matter, soil by soil.",r"The first panel ranks the ten soils by the change of a hydraulic property (the saturated conductivity or the water held) when one driver is moved from its present value. The bars are the sensitivity of the property to the driver; the scatter on the right places the soils on the plane of the two drivers. It is a direct reading of the van Genuchten parameters of Section~1.4 as functions of the pore structure.")
D[1]+=F('v7_1',r"Graphs of the drivers at your settings: drainage and water content against the consolidation rate, for the three tillage systems.",r"The graphs follow the drainage against the consolidation rate and the water content against the suction for conventional, reduced and no-till systems; clicking a point applies that setting to the whole program, so the effect of a change on the retention curve can be seen at once.")
D[1]+=F('v7_2',r"Real soil profiles: how tillage and settling change the soil, layer by layer, in the pictures of the model.",r"The pictures are sections through the topsoil of the ensemble runs after 0, 50, 100 and 200 days of settling. The dark cracks and macropores close as the soil consolidates, and the pore index printed under each section (0.92 and 0.67 for two states) is the number that enters the conductivity of the layer.")
D[2]=HEAD+r"The flow of water and the uptake by roots described in Chapter 2 are shown by the layer \emph{Root structure} as pictures of the real output of the Daisy ensemble: the roots on six dates of a season, the depth at which half of the uptake occurs, and the rooting depth of every soil."+"\n\n"
D[2]+=F('r1_0',r"Roots through the season: real model output, day by day, for the coarse loam in the weather of 1987.",r"The upper panel is the density of roots in the profile through the year (darker means more roots) and the lower panel the water taken by the roots (darker green is more uptake, blue is irrigation). The red line is the date chosen, here 16 April, when the roots reach 30 cm.")
D[2]+=F('r1_1',r"Root structure: where the roots are and where the water is taken, with the rooting depth of every year of the simulation.",r"The six drawings are the root system on six dates, and the curve below is the rooting depth in each of the 20 years of weather. The roots grow from 23 cm in January to 94 cm in July and are cut back at harvest, which is the pattern assumed by the root term $S(z)$ of the flow equation.")
D[2]+=F('r1_2',r"The day on the clock: the root density, the water taken today and the water taken in the year, at depth.",r"For the day chosen the figure gives the root density, the water taken that day (green) and the water taken over the year (blue), with the depth of half and of 90 per cent of the uptake marked (here 17.5 and 55 cm); the water is taken from the upper roots where the soil is wet.")
D[2]+=F('r1_3',r"Rooting depth in all ten soils, under conventional, reduced and no tillage, for the crop of the explorer.",r"The table gives the deepest roots and the depths of 50 and 90 per cent of the uptake for the ten soils, and the drawings show the roots of each soil on one day. Rooting depth differs by 11 cm among the soils (85 to 96 cm), and the model stops roots at 1 m.")
D[2]+=F('r1_4',r"What your rooting-depth runs show: 1\,000 simulations on the coarse loam, with the rooting depth from 30 to 180 cm.",r"The graphs show the yield, irrigation, drainage and other results against the rooting depth for 1\,000 runs, and the table gives the result of one run (roots of 120 cm, 6 mm per application). Deeper roots raise the yield slowly and reduce the nitrogen leached, but the gain vanishes below about 1.2 m.")
D[2]+=F('v2_0',r"Soil pressure head through the year, January to December, every year 2019 to 2038.",r"The coloured band is the pressure head at the sensor depth through the year for the 20 simulated years: it falls from field capacity in spring to the dry end of the range in summer. The vertical drawings at the right show the profile of the day.")
D[3]=HEAD+r"The trigger of Chapter 3 is shown in the application by two layers. \emph{Sensor and trigger} follows the suction at the sensor day by day through 20 seasons and marks the days beyond the trigger, and \emph{Trigger law} shows the depletion of the root zone against the trigger and the trade-off between a drier and a wetter trigger."+"\n\n"
D[3]+=F('v1_0',r"Sensor suction, every day from 2019 to 2039, with the trigger (dashed) and the irrigation events.",r"The line is the daily suction at the sensor depth, the dashed line is the trigger suction (here 471 cm for 50 per cent depletion) and the vertical marks are the irrigations. The bars below count the days beyond the trigger in each year.")
D[3]+=F('v1_1',r"One season at the sensor: 81 of 183 days beyond 471 cm and a mean suction of 378 cm, in the irrigated run.",r"The season is replayed day by day; the text gives the days beyond the trigger, the mean suction and the irrigation and yield of the run, which are the quantities that define the reach of a trigger.")
D[3]+=F('n1_0',r"Root-zone depletion and the trigger law: for the soils of the ensemble, the water held between field capacity and wilting, and the trigger at which irrigation fires.",r"The picture on the left is the root zone with the depletion $f$ coloured; the graph shows the suction and the depletion of the season against the trigger line; and the small drawings give the physical meaning of the numbers, namely that at a trigger of $f=0.50$ the suction is 471 cm in this soil.")
D[3]+=F('n1_1',r"The core trade-off: trigger at $f=0.50$ or $f=0.45$?",r"The table compares two neighbouring triggers on the yield, the irrigation, the drainage and the nitrogen leached, so that the cost of irrigating slightly earlier can be read directly. It is the practical use of the dose law of this chapter.")
D[4]=HEAD+r"The audit of Chapter 4 is the layer \emph{Full water balance}: every flux of the year, the 20-year means and the closure of the balance are drawn from the output of the model and not from a calculation by hand."+"\n\n"
D[4]+=F('wb2_0',r"Full water balance, year by year: rain, irrigation, evapotranspiration, drainage and the change of storage for each of the 20 years.",r"Each column is a year; the stacked bars split the outflow into evaporation, transpiration, interception, runoff and drainage, and the line is the input. The two sides of the balance are equal to within the residual printed below the chart, which is the closure error of Section~4.2.")
D[4]+=F('wb2_1',r"Evapotranspiration split into all its parts (20-year mean): the parts add up to the simulated total.",r"The bars split the evapotranspiration into transpiration, soil evaporation, interception and the evaporation of the mulch; the sum is checked against the model total.")
D[4]+=F('wb2_2',r"How the water enters and leaves the soil: 20-year means.",r"The final figure is the mean balance of the 20 years, a single picture of where the water of the field goes and the number to compare with a measured balance.")
D[5]=HEAD+r"Two layers show evapotranspiration and the crop requirement. \emph{Evaporation} follows the water from the pore to the atmosphere, and \emph{Crop water requirement} gives the net irrigation of each crop of the library on the soil of the explorer."+"\n\n"
D[5]+=F('e1_0',r"Evaporation from the pore to the atmosphere: the modelled section of the soil, the evaporation rate and the cumulative loss through the drying spell.",r"The picture on the left is the modelled soil in section, with its air-filled pores (white) and water-filled pores (blue); the graph shows the evaporation rate falling after wetting, from the first stage controlled by the atmosphere to the second controlled by the soil, and the cumulative loss.")
D[5]+=F('e1_1',r"Core-scale evaporation: from the matrix to the atmosphere, for the settings of the user.",r"Six panels follow the flux in the soil, the suction, the water content and the pore-size distribution of the surface layer for the conventional setting and for the reduced and no-till settings.")
D[5]+=F('e1_2',r"Conventional (your setting): where the water moves, and the suction profile, day 0.7.",r"The sections give the water content and the direction of the flow on day 0.7; vapour leaves upward in the dry layer and liquid water moves up from below in the film of the pores.")
D[5]+=F('crop_0',r"Crop water requirement: the crop, the net irrigation of the season and the daily need in the weather of the record.",r"The page begins with the crop and its coefficients and gives the net irrigation of the season for the soil and weather of the explorer, computed with $K_c\,ET_0$ and the trigger of the soil.")
D[5]+=F('crop_1',r"Root-zone water and the irrigation schedule: depletion below field capacity, with irrigation and rain events of 2026.",r"The depletion of the root zone is drawn against the readily available water; each irrigation returns it to field capacity, and the dates of the events are the schedule of the season.")
D[6]=HEAD+r"The design mathematics of Chapter 6 rests on weather and field data; \emph{Reliability over 20 seasons} shows how a design that works in one year behaves in all of them."+"\n\n"
D[6]+=F('yby_0',r"Year by year: how reliable is this setting? Yearly values of irrigation, drainage, evapotranspiration and yield.",r"For each of the 20 weather years the layer gives the irrigation, the drainage, the days of stress and the yield; a design is reliable if the yearly values stay within the band.")
D[6]+=F('yby_1',r"Evapotranspiration (mm per year) and days of crop water stress per season, across the 20 years.",r"The bars show the spread of two quantities that govern the design: the yearly crop water use and the stress days, which are the basis of the percentiles used for the design discharge and the capacity.")
D[6]+=F('yby_2',r"Reliability of this setting over the 20 simulated seasons from the yearly values above.",r"The summary gives the share of the seasons in which the setting meets the target without stress, a direct reading of the reliability concept of the design chapter.")
D[7]=HEAD+r"The layer \emph{Soil, plant and air} is the picture of the whole chain that Chapter 7 describes in words: one continuous water column from the groundwater to the atmosphere, in which the suction rises from $-0.02$ MPa in the soil to $-56$ MPa in the air."+"\n\n"
D[7]+=F('spac_0',r"Soil to sky: one continuous water column, with the suction, the water content and the pores holding water at each height.",r"Each height of the column from the water table to the leaves has its own suction and content; the arrows show the direction of the flow.")
D[7]+=F('spac_1',r"The air pulls with $-56$ MPa; the soil holds on with $-0.02$ MPa.",r"The figure compares the pull of the atmosphere on the water with the hold of the soil on it and shows that the water moves along the gradient of the total head.")
D[7]+=F('spac_2',r"Simulated suction, water content and pores holding water along the column.",r"The three profiles are the simulated state of the column on the day of the replay.")
D[8]=HEAD+r"The engines of Chapter 8 are controlled from the dashboard of the application, and the dashboard shows their results in four tabs: the \emph{Results} table, the \emph{Design} picture, the \emph{Charts} and the \emph{Messages} of the run."+"\n\n"
D[8]+=F('dash_results',r"The Dashboard, Results tab: the picture of the field with the water fluxes and the numbers of the run.",r"The picture shows the soil, the crop, the mulch and the fluxes of the season; the numbers at the right give the irrigation, the drainage, the evaporation and the yield.",w='1.0')
D[8]+=F('dash_charts',r"The Dashboard, Charts tab: the daily water balance and the depletion of the root zone.",r"The charts show the daily balance of the bucket engine of this chapter: rain, irrigation, evapotranspiration and drainage with the depletion against the trigger.",w='1.0')
D[8]+=F('dash_design',r"The Dashboard, Design tab: the design of the selected irrigation layer.",r"The Design tab shows the layout of the selected design with the numbers of the engine.",w='1.0')
D[8]+=F('dash_messages',r"The Dashboard, Messages tab: the log of the run, with the warnings of the engine.",r"Every step of the run is written in the message log, so that the user can see which engine ran and what it found.",w='1.0')
D[9]=HEAD+r"Four layers show the effect of structure, tillage and mulch: the microscope view of the topsoil, the comparison of a mulched and a bare soil, the feedback loops of the whole field and the fate of the water saved by the mulch."+"\n\n"
D[9]+=F('v3_0',r"Tillage and structure of the topsoil, under the microscope: modelled thin sections of the coarse loam for several tillage systems.",r"The sections are drawn from the pore-size distribution of the model; the white grains are sand, the blue spaces are the pores holding water and the dark spaces are the air-filled pores.")
D[9]+=F('v3_1',r"Relative pore space after tillage, and the change in drainage at the current settings, coarse loam.",r"The bars compare the pore space of the three tillage systems and the change of drainage; consolidation closes the large pores and lowers the drainage.")
D[9]+=F('v4_0',r"Mulched against bare soil: evaporation, drainage and transpiration of the two surfaces.",r"The two columns show the soil with and without mulch and the bars give the evaporation, transpiration, irrigation, drainage and nitrogen leached of each.")
D[9]+=F('v4_1',r"Your setting against bare soil: the saving of each flux.",r"The line gives the difference between the setting of the user and bare soil in each flux.")
D[9]+=F('v5_0',r"Feedback loops between climate, management, soil water, crop and nitrogen: the red arrows join the parts of the field that act on each other.",r"The drawing is the picture of the loops of Chapter 9: rain and temperature, irrigation, the soil surface and its mulch, the crop, the drainage and the nitrogen, with the actual fluxes of the run (irrigation 250 mm, evaporation 172 mm, drainage 21 mm).")
D[9]+=F('v5_1',r"What one step of each control does (click a row or a numbered arrow).",r"Each row gives the effect of one step of one control (a $+0.5\,^\circ$C change, 10 per cent more rain, a stronger mulch) on the yield, the irrigation and the leaching.")
D[9]+=F('v5_2',r"Hidden questions of the vadose zone, answered for the coarse loam: sensors, compaction, the water table (questions 1 to 4).",r"Each panel is a question answered by sections and graphs of the model; for example the wetting and drying of the pores or the reach of the water table into the root zone.")
D[9]+=F('v5_3',r"Hidden questions of the vadose zone (questions 7 and 8): where on the suction scale the trigger really falls, and how long the soil keeps up with the sun.",r"The last two panels connect the trigger to the pores that are emptied at that suction and the time that the soil delivers water to the surface.")
D[9]+=F('n2_0',r"Fate of the water saved by mulch.",r"The figure divides the water that the mulch saves into less irrigation, more drainage, more transpiration and storage.")
D[9]+=F('n2_1',r"Fate of saved water: less irrigation 38 mm, more drainage 13 mm, more transpiration 17 mm.",r"The numbers are the fate of the saved millimetres: they are not all kept for the crop, and the panel follows the shares.")
D[9]+=F('n3_0',r"Regime in the soil profile: vapour leaves upward, drainage downward, for rainfall scales from dry to wet.",r"The five soil columns are the profile for five rainfall scales; the arrows show the evaporation and drainage and the colour bar gives the index that classifies the regime as drainage-dominated or evaporation-dominated.")
D[10]=HEAD+r"The phenotyping layers have two pictures: the deterministic plant of the model and the volumetric scan of a real plant by a camera that turns round it."+"\n\n"
D[10]+=F('dph_0',r"Deterministic phenotyping: the plant of the model through the season.",r"The layer draws the plant of the model for the chosen crop and day, with the height, the leaf area index, the roots and the cover.")
D[10]+=F('dph_1',r"Run simulation: the phenotype for the settings of the user.",r"After the run the numbers of the plant (height, LAI, biomass) are given for the days of the season.")
D[10]+=F('vph_0',r"Volumetric phenotyping: the plant, the camera rig and the six steps of the scan.",r"The page is a guided tour of the six steps from the rig to the measured volume.")
D[10]+=F('vph_1',r"Step 2: the camera turns round the plant; the views arrive and are joined.",r"The arm turns, a view arrives at each position and the views are joined into one cloud of points.")
D[10]+=F('vph_2',r"Step 2 of 6 (the camera turns round the plant), with the moving simulation of the scan.",r"The simulation can be played step by step to see the point cloud grow.")
D[10]+=F('vph_3',r"Step 3: growth from emergence to the end of the season.",r"The same rig scans the plant on seven dates of the season to follow its growth.")
D[10]+=F('vph_4',r"Step 5: volume and form measured on the joined cloud against the model for the same day.",r"The volume of the point cloud (voxel counting) is compared with the model value, an error check of the method.")
D[10]+=F('vph_5',r"Step 7: through the season, a scan every ten days (24 of 24 done); the line is the model.",r"The measured volumes form the growth curve and the line is the deterministic model.")
D[10]+=F('vph_6',r"Step 8: what feeds this layer and what it feeds.",r"The last panel states the inputs of the layer and the numbers that depend on it.")
for n,t in D.items(): open(OUT+'app%02d.tex'%n,'w').write(t)
print('ok')
